  "final_text": "I finished all four steps, and reading the dashboard and the rule back shows they are set up as asked.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) uses `timestamp` as its time field. It also has `@timestamp` and `utc_time` date fields, but those aren't the configured time field. The response code is stored as text, in `response` / `response.keyword`, not as a number.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-3cf09366 Web logs' with two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of request count per time interval.\n   - A bar chart of request count by `response.keyword`, showing the top 20 codes.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-3cf09366 5xx errors' as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, using `timestamp` for time. It runs every minute and fires when there are more than 10 matching requests in the last 5 minutes. A request matches when `response.keyword` is from \"500\" to \"599\". Because the code is stored as text, this compares the values as text rather than as numbers. That gives the right result for normal three-digit codes like these. The rule was created already enabled, so it didn't need enabling.\n4. **Panel edit and check:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\" and confirmed the new title on the dashboard. When I read the rule back it showed `enabled: true` with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
